&lt;p&gt;I Built an Interactive 3D Anatomy Explorer Using React, TypeScript and Three.js&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've recently completed a project I've been working on called Anatomy Explorer, formerly known as Wildlife Anatomy Museum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was to build something that makes exploring animal and human anatomy more interactive. Rather than presenting information through ordinary images and text alone, I wanted to create an experience where visitors could explore 3D models, examine different anatomical structures and learn through interaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the project offers&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anatomy Explorer brings together different animal species and the human body in one interactive environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of the main features include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A gallery of different animal and human specimens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Interactive 3D models that users can rotate and zoom.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Anatomical layers and structures where supported by the available models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Information panels with details about selected specimens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A responsive interface for different screen sizes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was to make the experience useful for anyone interested in anatomy, including students, biology enthusiasts and people who simply want to explore how different organisms are structured.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technologies I used&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built the application with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TypeScript&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vite&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Three.js&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React Three Fiber&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drei&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Font Awesome&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is deployed on Netlify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some thoughts behind the development&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing I wanted to achieve with this project was to move beyond building conventional websites and explore how interactive 3D technology could be used in practical web applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Working with React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript gave me an opportunity to explore a different side of frontend development. It also helped me understand how to bring together multiple technologies to create an application that offers more than a static user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is always room to improve, and I see this project as another important step in developing my skills and becoming better at building useful software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore Anatomy Explorer&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live Website: &lt;a href="https://wildlife-anatomy-museum.netlify.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://wildlife-anatomy-museum.netlify.app/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd genuinely appreciate feedback from developers, biology students, educators and anyone interested in interactive learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feel free to explore the application and share your thoughts. I'd be interested to hear what you think and what you would improve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My project Vesper originally started from a motion design I found on Dribbble for a smart home integration company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I really liked the animation and started thinking about how I could turn it into a fully functional website and use it as a personal showcase project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My main focus with Vesper was high-quality design smooth animations and interactive experiences. I built the website using React and GSAP.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more really cool feature that I implemented is a interactive smart home demo where the visitors can interacts with rooms and change lightings and shading presets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live website: &lt;a href="https://vesper.aporka.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://vesper.aporka.dev/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Original inspiration: &lt;a href="https://dribbble.com/shots/27231469-Luxury-Smart-Living-Website-Animation-Experience?utm_source=chatgpt.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://dribbble.com/shots/27231469-Luxury-Smart-Living-Website-Animation-Experience?utm_source=chatgpt.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raise your hand if you've ever waited for a full redeploy because another team changed one button in the header. 🙋&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the whole pitch for micro-frontends. Every team ships its own piece of the page whenever it wants, and the page just... picks it up. No coordinated releases. No "can you merge my PR before Friday?".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Next.js, the go-to tool for this was &lt;code&gt;@module-federation/nextjs-mf&lt;/code&gt;. Then the App Router arrived, and nextjs-mf never followed. So I did what developers do when a tool doesn't exist: I built one. 😅&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's called &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;next-federation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, it's open source, and it just hit 0.2. Here's how it works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  🧩 Two ways to borrow someone else's UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick vocabulary first. The app that &lt;em&gt;provides&lt;/em&gt; the UI is the &lt;strong&gt;remote&lt;/strong&gt;. Your Next.js app that &lt;em&gt;renders&lt;/em&gt; it is the &lt;strong&gt;host&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote can hand you UI in two ways.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Islands.&lt;/strong&gt; The remote renders React Server Components on its own server and sends back HTML. Your page embeds it, and it hydrates as its own little React root. The nice part: the remote's code never runs on your server. It keeps its own secrets, its own database and its own problems. 😄&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remote components.&lt;/strong&gt; The remote ships a client component (Module Federation 2.0 under the hood), and it renders right inside your React tree. It can read your context and use your router, so it behaves like it was always there. Great for things like a cart or a nav menu.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Islands&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Remote components&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Remote's code runs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;On the remote's server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In your React tree&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Props&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plain JSON&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anything&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your React context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Doesn't cross in&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Readable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good for&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Content the remote owns&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Interactive bits that must feel native&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And yes, you can mix both on the same page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  ⚡ Your first island in about five minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The remote
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote is just a small Vite project. You tell it which islands it serves and who's allowed to embed them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts (remote)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nextFederationIslands&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/vite'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'vite'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;nextFederationIslands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'shop'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://shop.example.com/'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;hosts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.example.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;islands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./src/islands/ProductPanel.tsx'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;An island is an async Server Component. Fetch whatever you need, drop in client components, the usual:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/islands/ProductPanel.tsx (remote)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'../data'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./Counter'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;${product.price}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  The host
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the Next.js side there are three small pieces. Wrap your config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// next.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;withNextFederation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/next/config'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;withNextFederation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;List the remote and create the island component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// lib/federation.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'server-only'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineFederation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/core'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createIslands&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/next/server'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;federation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineFederation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;remotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;shop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;origin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://shop.example.com'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createIslands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;federation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And from there it's just a component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'../lib/federation'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;remote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shop"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;island&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ProductPanel"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'A1'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;errorFallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now when the shop team deploys a new &lt;code&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/code&gt;, your page shows it. You don't rebuild anything. And if their server is having a bad day, your page still returns &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and shows the &lt;code&gt;errorFallback&lt;/code&gt; instead of falling over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  🗄️ Caching is where I was most stubborn
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Islands go through Next.js's own fetch cache. So a page with a public island can stay completely static, or use ISR, like any other page. Need one island refreshed right now? &lt;code&gt;revalidateIsland()&lt;/code&gt; calls &lt;code&gt;revalidateTag&lt;/code&gt; under the hood. On Next 16 it works with &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/code&gt; too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I set myself one rule early and kept it: the library must never quietly turn a static page into a dynamic one. If you've ever lost an afternoon to "why is this page suddenly not cached?!", you know why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  🔐 Per-user islands, without leaking tokens
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Islands can also be &lt;strong&gt;private&lt;/strong&gt;, meaning they render per signed-in user. After a &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/guides/identity" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;short identity setup&lt;/a&gt;, the host mints a short-lived token for the remote, and the remote reads the user with &lt;code&gt;getIdentity()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That token never shows up in your HTML, in Next.js's RSC payload or in browser storage. And I'm not just hoping that's true: the e2e suite checks for it, in &lt;code&gt;next dev&lt;/code&gt; and in production builds.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shop"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;island&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"MyOrders"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  🛡️ When does the host trust a remote's code?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part I thought about the most. If your server renders a remote component, it's running someone else's code on your machine. So the host only does that when the build is &lt;strong&gt;signed&lt;/strong&gt; (or pinned by digest) and every file matches its sha384 hash. Anything else renders in the browser only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A small confession: while I was preparing 0.2, one test kept failing about half the time. It turned out that, with the right timing, the runtime could load the wrong build of a remote when two builds shared a name. Not fun to track down. But I'd much rather CI finds that than you. 😬&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Remote components can also use &lt;code&gt;next/link&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;next/navigation&lt;/code&gt; or React Router. The build rewires those imports, so the same component navigates through whatever router the host has. App Router, Pages Router, a React Router SPA, it doesn't care.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  🤷 Why only public Next.js APIs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because I really don't want to rewrite this every time Next.js ships a release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything goes through APIs that Next.js documents: &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; with tags, &lt;code&gt;'use cache'&lt;/code&gt;, Route Handlers and &lt;code&gt;unstable_rethrow&lt;/code&gt;. There are no &lt;code&gt;next/dist&lt;/code&gt; imports, and CI fails if one sneaks in. It's a bet that a Next.js upgrade won't break your micro-frontends. Ask me again in a year. 😄&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  👀 Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The live demo is probably the fastest way to get the idea: three hosts (App Router, Pages Router and a React SPA) pulling UI from two separately deployed remotes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live demo:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/live-demo" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/live-demo&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Docs:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation&lt;/a&gt; (if you like it, a star really helps ⭐)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's 0.2, so there are rough edges. If you've tried micro-frontends with Next.js before, I'd genuinely like to know what got in your way. Drop it in the comments or open an issue!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;next-federation is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by Vercel.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What carries over
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Vue concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;React equivalent&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How it helps you transition&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Component‑based UI&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Function or class components&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both frameworks break the UI into reusable, isolated units. Your habit of thinking in “components that own their template and logic” maps directly to React’s JSX‑based components.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Props for data flow&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;props&lt;/code&gt; (read‑only)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In Vue you pass data with &lt;code&gt;:prop="value"&lt;/code&gt;; in React you use &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MyComp value={value} /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The unidirectional flow is the same mental model.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Local state&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; (hooks) or &lt;code&gt;this.state&lt;/code&gt; (class)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; object becomes component state. The rule “state is private to the component unless lifted” stays unchanged.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Computed / derived values&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; or derived values inside render&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;computed&lt;/code&gt; properties are just memoized functions. In React you can achieve the same with &lt;code&gt;useMemo(() =&amp;gt; …, [deps])&lt;/code&gt; or by computing directly in the render function when cheap.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lifecycle hooks&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (hooks) or class lifecycle methods&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;updated&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;beforeDestroy&lt;/code&gt; correspond to &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … }, [])&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … })&lt;/code&gt;, and cleanup functions.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Event handling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;v-on&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; etc.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The pattern “emit an event upward” becomes calling a prop callback (&lt;code&gt;onClick={() =&amp;gt; props.onSave()}&lt;/code&gt;) or using a context/store.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scoped CSS&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CSS Modules / styled‑components&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; isolates styles; in React you can use CSS Modules (&lt;code&gt;import styles from './Comp.module.css'&lt;/code&gt;) or CSS‑in‑JS libraries to get the same encapsulation.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Single‑file component mindset&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Separate &lt;code&gt;.jsx/.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and style files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;While Vue bundles template, script, and style, the idea of co‑locating a component’s logic, markup, and styles persists; you’ll just split them into files that are imported together.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reactivity basics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Immutable state updates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s reactivity system automatically tracks dependencies; React requires you to treat state as immutable and call the setter to trigger a re‑render. The conceptual goal—UI reflects state—remains identical.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Testing approach&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;vue-test-utils&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;@testing-library/react&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both encourage shallow rendering and interaction testing. Your test‑first mindset transfers without friction.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What's new
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Side‑by‑side example: a toggle button
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vue (single‑file component)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight vue"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;template&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;@click&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"toggle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ON'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'OFF'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/button&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/template&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;style&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;scoped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/style&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React (function component with hooks)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./ToggleButton.css'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// CSS Modules or plain CSS&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ToggleButton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setIsOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ON'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'OFF'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


  What changed and why
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Template vs JSX&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;template&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; uses HTML‑like syntax with directives (&lt;code&gt;@click&lt;/code&gt;). React embeds markup directly in JavaScript via JSX, so event handlers become &lt;code&gt;onClick={handler}&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State declaration&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; returns an object; React uses the &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; hook which returns a tuple &lt;code&gt;[value, setter]&lt;/code&gt;. The setter must be called to trigger a re‑render.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Methods vs inline functions&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue groups handlers under &lt;code&gt;methods&lt;/code&gt;. In React you can declare functions inside the component or use &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; for memoization; the example keeps it simple.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scoped styles&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; automatically scopes selectors. React relies on CSS Modules (&lt;code&gt;ToggleButton.module.css&lt;/code&gt;) or CSS‑in‑JS to achieve isolation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File structure&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue bundles everything in one &lt;code&gt;.vue&lt;/code&gt; file; React typically separates concerns (&lt;code&gt;.jsx&lt;/code&gt; for logic/markup, &lt;code&gt;.css&lt;/code&gt; for styles).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Other new concepts:  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – React’s functional paradigm revolves around hooks (&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;). They replace Vue’s options API and give you fine‑grained control over side effects.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;JSX is JavaScript&lt;/strong&gt; – You can use any JS expression inside &lt;code&gt;{}&lt;/code&gt;; no need for Vue’s template interpolation rules.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explicit imports&lt;/strong&gt; – Every component, hook, and utility must be imported; there is no global registration like Vue’s &lt;code&gt;app.component&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict immutability&lt;/strong&gt; – Updating nested state requires shallow copies (&lt;code&gt;setState(prev =&amp;gt; ({ ...prev, nested: newVal }))&lt;/code&gt;). Vue’s proxy‑based reactivity hides this, but React expects you to manage it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Common pitfalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pitfall&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it happens for Vue devs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How to avoid it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutating state directly&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s proxies let you write &lt;code&gt;this.foo = …&lt;/code&gt; anywhere. In React you must call the state setter.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always use the setter (&lt;code&gt;setFoo(newVal)&lt;/code&gt;). For objects/arrays, spread‑copy before updating.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting dependency arrays in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;watch&lt;/code&gt; runs only when watched sources change. In React an empty &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs once, but omitting the array makes it run on every render.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicitly pass &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; for “run once” or &lt;code&gt;[dep1, dep2]&lt;/code&gt; for specific dependencies.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming JSX auto‑escapes HTML&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;v-html&lt;/code&gt; is explicit; in React you must use &lt;code&gt;dangerouslySetInnerHTML&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;dangerouslySetInnerHTML&lt;/code&gt; only when necessary and sanitize input.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; in function components&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s options API relies on &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;. React function components have no &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep all logic inside the function scope or use hooks; never reference &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Over‑using context for simple prop drilling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s provide/inject is often used for deep trees. React’s Context has a higher render cost if misused.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reserve Context for truly global data; otherwise lift state up and pass props.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Neglecting key props in lists&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue warns about missing &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt;. React will silently reuse DOM nodes, causing bugs.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always provide a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;key={item.id}&lt;/code&gt;) when rendering arrays.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming CSS is scoped automatically&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; does the work. React does not.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use CSS Modules, styled‑components, or BEM naming to avoid collisions.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mixing default and named imports&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s default export (&lt;code&gt;export default&lt;/code&gt;) is common. React components are usually default‑exported, but utilities are named.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Follow the library’s export style; &lt;code&gt;import React, { useState } from 'react'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Your 30‑day plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Week 1 – Foundations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Set up the toolchain&lt;/strong&gt; – Install Node ≥ 18, run &lt;code&gt;npx create-react-app my-app&lt;/code&gt; (or Vite with &lt;code&gt;npm create vite@latest&lt;/code&gt;). Commit the scaffold to Git.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learn JSX basics&lt;/strong&gt; – Convert a simple Vue template (e.g., a list rendering) to JSX, paying attention to &lt;code&gt;{}&lt;/code&gt; interpolation and &lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State with &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; – Re‑implement a Vue component that toggles visibility using &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Build a &lt;strong&gt;Todo List&lt;/strong&gt; that adds, toggles, and deletes items. Use only functional components and &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Week 2 – Effects, Context, and Routing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Side effects&lt;/strong&gt; – Replace a Vue &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt; API call with &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … }, [])&lt;/code&gt;. Experiment with cleanup functions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React Router&lt;/strong&gt; – Install &lt;code&gt;react-router-dom&lt;/code&gt;, create a two‑page app (Home / About). Compare Vue Router’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;router-link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context API&lt;/strong&gt; – Move the Todo List’s state to a Context provider and consume it in a child component. Observe re‑renders.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Extend the Todo app with &lt;strong&gt;persistent storage&lt;/strong&gt; using &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. Add a settings page reachable via routing.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Week 3 – Advanced Patterns
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Custom hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – Extract the Todo logic into a &lt;code&gt;useTodos&lt;/code&gt; hook. Compare to Vue composables.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Performance&lt;/strong&gt; – Use &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; to prevent unnecessary renders in a list with many items.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Styling&lt;/strong&gt; – Switch from global CSS to CSS Modules or styled‑components; verify that styles are scoped.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Create a &lt;strong&gt;Dashboard&lt;/strong&gt; with a chart (e.g., using &lt;code&gt;recharts&lt;/code&gt;) and a data table. Use custom hooks for data fetching, memoization for expensive calculations, and Context for theme toggling.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Week 4 – Ecosystem &amp;amp; Production
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State management&lt;/strong&gt; – Explore a lightweight store like &lt;strong&gt;Zustand&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;Jotai&lt;/strong&gt;; compare to Vuex/Pinia. Implement a global counter.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Testing&lt;/strong&gt; – Write unit tests with &lt;strong&gt;@testing-library/react&lt;/strong&gt; for the Dashboard components; add a few integration tests with &lt;strong&gt;Cypress&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Build &amp;amp; Deploy&lt;/strong&gt; – Run &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt;, serve the static files locally, then deploy to a free static host (e.g., Netlify). Verify source maps and environment variables.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Polish the Dashboard, add error boundaries, and write a short README that explains the architecture.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By the end of the month you’ll have touched every core React concept, built three progressively larger apps, and exercised the tooling that surrounds a production React codebase.  &lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How different is the learning curve compared to moving from Vue 2 to Vue 3?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The shift to React introduces a new syntax (JSX) and a hook‑centric mental model, which is steeper than Vue’s optional Composition API but still manageable with daily practice.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Do I need to learn TypeScript for React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not immediately; React works perfectly with plain JavaScript. However, most modern React codebases adopt TypeScript, so consider adding it after you’re comfortable with the core concepts.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Should I keep using Vue‑style single‑file components?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React does not have an exact equivalent, but you can approximate the experience with tools like &lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;MDX&lt;/strong&gt;. Most teams prefer separate &lt;code&gt;.jsx/.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;.css&lt;/code&gt; files for clarity.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is React’s Context a replacement for Vuex/Pinia?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Context is good for low‑frequency global data (theme, locale). For complex state with actions, mutations, and devtools, a dedicated store (Zustand, Jotai, Redux) is more appropriate.&lt;/p&gt;

